#!/usr/bin/env bash
# READ-ONLY priority-first tick header for the coordinator seat (rule P1, .ai/coordinator-duties.md §3 and §9).
# Usage: scripts/coordinator/top5 [--seat <name>] [--ack | --rules-only]
#   --seat        legacy-coordinator-agent (default) or throughput-plans-coordinator
#   --ack         record the rules state the last run displayed (rule RULES-ACK) and exit 0
#   --rules-only  print only the RULES-CHANGED section, with the same exit status
# Exit status: 0 nothing unacknowledged; 3 RULES-CHANGED (unacknowledged rule changes, or
# no ack recorded); 4 a rules source could not be read and nothing read has changed.
# Rule state lives outside the repo: ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/livespec-coordinator/.
# Shell entry point; the logic is embedded Python (stdlib only) so the repo's
# product-Python AST gates, which govern the package, do not apply to a script.
set -euo pipefail
COORDINATOR_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
export COORDINATOR_DIR
exec python3 -I - "$@" <<'PY'
"""Priority-first tick header (coordinator 546979bb, 2026-11-09, after the 10.5 h stall of priority 1).

Run FIRST every tick, before any peer message is answered. For priorities 1-5 of the
latest priorities-record on epic livespec-dev-tooling-6tgd2z it prints the epic status,
its typed next_action, the age of its last ledger move (STALE past 60 minutes) and the
newest epic comment, so an ask from a seat that cannot SendMessage (a Mac or Codex
seat) is seen every tick (rule P3). (The 2026-11-20 record ranks P1-P7; TOP lists them all.)

TOP is only the FALLBACK (printed after an UNREAD priorities-record line) and the source of the KEPT rows; the P-list is parsed from the newest PRIORITIES now scope event on epic livespec-dev-tooling-6tgd2z. A KEPT row is a standing seat, not a ranked priority. When the record changes, change TOP in the same
docs PR; the record wins over this list until then. Nothing here mutates: only
`bd show` and `bd comments`, through each tenant's credential wrapper, and local reads
of the launch hold files.

The HOLDS section (rule HOLD-ENDS-ARE-DRIVEN, coordinator 2026-11-13, after hold-gmktec
stood more than 3 h past the release of its fix) prints every live hold with its age,
marks one older than 60 minutes STALE HOLD, and marks one with no OWNER: line or no
END CONDITION as MALFORMED HOLD. A hold carrying a line that starts with `DRAINED:` (after
leading whitespace), such as `DRAINED: <plan> off the priorities record as of 2026-11-18`, or
whose END CONDITION says it was drained under the 2026-11-14 ~17:5xZ shutdown directive (the
legacy marker), prints DRAINED with its END CONDITION snippet and is not counted STALE: it
ends only when a priorities-record re-adds its plan, and about 30 of them printed STALE every
tick buried the real stalls (coordinator 2026-11-15).

The OVERCOMMIT section (maintainer-approved 2026-11-13) prints, first in the header, every
node fleet-utilization.sh --overcommit-only marks FLAGGED (idle under 5 % with the BestEffort
CI slice's CPU pressure above 30: the classes sharing the node exceed its CPU) or UNREAD, for
routing to the per-node CPU budget owner the same tick.

The FROZEN-SEATS section (rule PERMISSION-PROMPT, after the 2026-11-20 fleet freeze of about 14 h at a Remote
Control "Enter to select" dialog) prints before everything else: every herdr seat on this and every saved machine
whose last 12 screen lines carry the dialog footer ("Enter to select" with "Esc"), read with `herdr pane read`
(it types nothing). It skips the seat running top5 and resume-updates, bounds each call to 20 s and the section to
60 s, and prints UNREAD for what it could not read. An IDLE-FLEET line follows: the oldest last turn end over 60 min.

The RULES-CHANGED section (rule RULES-ACK, coordinator-duties §3; after the 2026-11-16
RULES-REREAD records, when a seat acted on a stale copy of its own rules) prints FIRST,
before INCIDENT MODE. Since the seat's last `--ack` it shows the diff of
.ai/coordinator-duties.md at origin/master, the diff of the seat's charter file(s), and every
new comment on the seat's watched epics whose first line carries SCOPE, MAINTAINER, RULE or
VERBATIM: at most 80 lines in all, each comment cut at 600 characters. When anything is
unacknowledged the script still prints its full header, then exits 3. `--ack` records the
state the last run displayed, so a change that lands between reading and acking stays
pending. Its only writes are the per-seat state files under the user's state directory and
the `git fetch` of origin/master.
"""
